#!/opt/dsl41/.venv/bin/python -I
"""dsl41-release -- release or roll back the nightbank estate with one command.

An example the site package owns, installed beside dsl41-launch (DL-218,
DL-407). It runs dsl41's own verbs in order and records what it did; the
runner's lineage stays the truth. Every path and run option comes from the
launcher's CONFIGURATION through `dsl41-launch vars`, so this file holds
none. The first line names the launcher's PREFIX: a site that changes PREFIX
changes both. It imports nothing but the standard library and the installed
dsl41 package.

  dsl41-release prepare NAME SOURCE_DIR
      Copy SOURCE_DIR, which holds estate.toml, to ESTATE_RELEASES/NAME
      through NAME.new. Lint and rehearse it with the installed runtime, the
      launcher's ENVIRONMENT and the site's PROPERTIES; rehearse plays one
      day from now, its default. Refuse it when either exits nonzero, when
      it holds more jobs than MAX_JOBS, or when the runtime's command grace
      is above MAX_GRACE_S. The grace is the runtime's, not a job's: dsl41
      has no per-job grace. A prepared release holds .release/prepared.json
      with the sha256 of each of its files, and its inputs are read-only. A
      refused one is removed. prepared.json carries no bundle hash: no dsl41
      verb prints one before a seal stages the bundle, so the seal and the
      apply's receipt carry it.
  dsl41-release prepare --requalify NAME
      Run the same checks on the installed release under the current runtime,
      its files unchanged, and replace prepared.json when they pass. An apply
      refuses a release prepared under another dsl41 version until then.
  dsl41-release apply [--resume] NAME
      Release NAME into the running estate (deployment-runbook ss0's job
      recipe, ss6a). It refuses unless the engine answers and NAME's files
      are the ones prepare checked. Then it holds every scheduled top-level
      job `query timers` lists that is not held, and waits until no job is
      STARTING, RUNNING or QUE_WAIT. A job the release changes or removes
      that is still armed or has a pending timer refuses the apply, as the
      recipe requires; the seal itself would let a changed one cross with a
      recorded assumption (period-model ss10.3).
      It seals from NAME's manifest, writes ACTIVE, waits for the sealed
      engine to exit and starts the engine unit. It checks that the
      successor answers in the committed period, under a new leader, from
      the bundle the seal committed and NAME's files. It releases the holds
      it took and writes NAME/.release/applied-<request id>.json, which
      NAME/.release/latest names. A seal refused inside the retry horizon is
      sent again, under a new request id, once the horizon it names has
      passed (period-model ss9).
  dsl41-release rollback
      Apply the release the active release's latest receipt names as its
      previous one, the same way; with none, use `apply NAME`.
  dsl41-release status [--format text|json]
      The active release, each release's receipts, and a recorded apply with
      what `apply --resume` would do.

An apply holds ESTATE_RELEASES/.apply.lock, so a second one, or a rollback,
is refused, and records each step in ESTATE_RELEASES/.apply.json before it
acts. A stopped apply, an interrupted one included, resumes from that record
and never from ACTIVE. Every seal it sends claims one actor, recorded with
the apply: the spelling the engine records for the service account
(access-model ss3), so a retry is the same envelope live or offline. Before
it sends a seal again under a recorded request id, and after any seal that
did not exit 0, it reads the estate for that id: a seal record at the end
of a WAL segment, with its sidecar (deployment-runbook ss6a, Day 2). A
committed seal is never sent again, and a retry never takes a fresh id.
A hold is the helper's when the helper's own request applied it. The
request id is composed and recorded before the send, so a resume sends a
pending hold again under that id, and the engine answers from its first
decision; an applied hold that moved no revision held nothing new and is
not the helper's. The helper releases each of its holds that is still held,
a hold a schedule tick latched included, under a request id recorded before
the send, so a release with no decision is sent again under that id. It
keeps a hold when the run root's log shows an OFF_HOLD of the job from
another request after the helper's hold: the hold there now is somebody
else's. It never releases a hold it did not apply. DSL41_RELEASE_STOP_AFTER=seal-reply kills the
helper after the seal exits 0 and before that is recorded; =seal kills it
after it is recorded and before ACTIVE is written. The drill uses both, and
nothing else should.

Identity. Run it as the deployment identity, which owns ESTATE_RELEASES and
ACTIVE and may replace ACTIVE in its directory. It sends `query` and
`sendevent` itself, so the access map must bind it to `ops`, and it must be
a member of the map's socket_group (access-model ss4, ss10). Lint, rehearse,
the seal and the reads inside the run root run as the service account, as
this file's `service` mode through `sudo -u dsl41 -H`, invoked by the path
this file was run as. That mode takes release names, a request id and the
recorded actor, never a command line. Its seal refuses, exit 2, a release
that is not prepared under this runtime or whose files changed since, as an
apply does, so the grant seals nothing prepare did not check; otherwise it
exits with `dsl41 seal`'s own code. The start runs through `sudo systemctl start dsl41-engine.service`.
dsl41-release.sudoers beside this file grants those two commands and nothing
else.

Exit codes:
  0  done
  2  refused, or the seal did not commit: no apply is recorded, ACTIVE is
     unchanged, and the holds this command took are released
  3  stopped with the apply recorded: `status` says what
     `apply --resume NAME` does
  4  the seal's outcome is unknown: the apply is recorded with its request
     id, and `apply --resume NAME` reads the estate for it first
"""

